--TEST--
`Core\Socket::upgrade`'s `limits:` is the connection's own ceiling, as `spawn script`'s is a child's: the connection is stopped where its own limit is, and the same allocation succeeds in the request under the server's wider one — `rule:concurrency/an-upgrades-options-are-spawn-scripts`, `rule:concurrency/a-connection-is-a-root-isolate` and `rule:errors/on-limit`
--HEADERS--
Upgrade: websocket
Connection: Upgrade
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.script]
spawn = true

# Wide enough that what stops the connection is its own `limits:` alone.
[limits]
memory = "256M"
--FILE greedy.nvs--
<?nvs
echo "connection started\n";
array<int> $rows = [];
for (var $i = 0; $i < 400000; $i = $i + 1) {
    $rows[] = $i;
}
echo "unreachable\n";
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The connection starts when the request ends. Its client sees `1011`, and its
// last line never prints.
Core\Socket::upgrade("greedy.nvs", limits: {memory: "1M"});

// The request is held to the server's limit, not the connection's.
array<int> $rows = [];
for (var $i = 0; $i < 400000; $i = $i + 1) {
    $rows[] = $i;
}
echo "request held ", Core\Arr::count($rows), "\n";
--EXPECT--
request held 400000
closed: 1011 this connection's isolate ended in a failure
connection started
